17] [17592186159056 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159056 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e3c90ebe-b224-55df-b9c4-1fdf615053da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159057 #edb/ref 1080 "0.35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159057 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159057 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8bd57af1-7d1c-5a33-86ee-75a99adf862f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159058 #edb/ref 1080 "0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159058 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159058 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c4949a14-dd68-5870-8eac-ee6f1946e222" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159059 #edb/ref 1080 "0.65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159059 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159059 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "083e837b-2ec9-5df2-ba3d-d1f0563aad88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159060 #edb/ref 1080 "0.55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159060 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159060 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "272dc244-3662-559a-9f8a-dbaebde63ac7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159061 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159061 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159061 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "599a2b1b-7775-57a1-bb2d-ec733f0c182b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161668 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161668 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find the marginal mass function for $X.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "162ebeba-e9ee-5588-8d79-659754a77548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161668 #edb/ref 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.3}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.7}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, the marginal mass function for $X$ is given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161871 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9189" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161871 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(X = 0).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da693f0e-db20-5a7f-9737-3f26d5d6581c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161871 #edb/ref 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{12}}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{12}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & ={f}_{X}(0) \\\\  & =f(0,0)+f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161872 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161872 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(X \\geq 2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e99dc081-a5d6-5770-a0ea-da172b0d37f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161872 #edb/ref 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.5}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $X$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =0.5+0.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Marginal Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162071 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "96760d15-5e56-529b-a5da-1697dc782031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Cumulative Probability Given a Joint Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162072 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "96473ec4-67f8-5614-b187-0961062aac29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Marginal Probability Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162102 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ac80eed8-d801-5275-9bd1-73ba43fadb38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1045 "Marginal Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162163 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2698ccf6-18f8-573f-9adf-9c8bdcc0f6e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162309 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162309 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162309 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y = 1$ | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, calculate $P(Y \\le 2)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162309 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "040f74e5-7507-505e-befe-d132e8cdef58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162990 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162990 #edb/ref 1061 34052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162990 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0dce6d66-b5fe-52cc-ae58-8918d96eb144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162990 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162997 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162997 #edb/ref 1061 34274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162997 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b36fe44c-1d35-5f90-8f95-9d5746329173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162997 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162998 #edb/ref 1061 34277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162998 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e2655db-a7c3-5298-b5ab-6b47cfe4c0e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162998 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163063 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163063 #edb/ref 1061 37907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163063 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f514a8f7-d466-5f40-8ce4-7fcfebb6e28c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163063 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163082 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163082 #edb/ref 1061 38105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163082 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83e1320a-f77b-5173-bedf-f02c9d400247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163082 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163083 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163083 #edb/ref 1061 38106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163083 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "97117126-1ac4-57aa-9c23-c3e882c06372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163083 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163281 #edb/ref 1064 "The joint PMF $f(x,y)$ of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ tells us all possible events $(X, Y)$ and the probabilities associated with each event. However, suppose we wish to determine the probability mass function for $X$ only. How can this be deduced from the joint PMF?\n\nLet's build some intuition by considering an example.\n\nConsider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X={\\color{blue}{1}}$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X={\\color{red}{2}}$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nThe support of $X$ is $S_X = \\{{\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{2}}\\}.$ Let's now deduce the probabilities associated with each value of $X$ in $S_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- According to the law of total probability, we can deduce $P(X=1)$ by summing the values in the first row of the table, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =P(X=1,Y=1)+P(X=1,Y=2) \\\\  & =0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we summed over all possible values of $Y$ associated with $X=1.$\n\n- Similarly, by summing the values in the second row in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =P(X=2,Y=1)+P(X=2,Y=2) \\\\  & =0.7+0.1 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we summed over all possible values of $Y$ associated with $X=2.$\n\nTherefore, the probability mass function for $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n\nNotice that we have used the notation $f_X(x).$ Since $f_X(x)$ was deduced from the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ we call $f_X(x)$ the **marginal mass function** for $X.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163281 #edb/ref 1065 5469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163281 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0281a245-2f6d-5a89-8ea7-5d77bf3dca4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163281 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163331 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's go back to the last example for the random variables $X$ and $Y.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe saw that the marginal mass function $f_X$ for $X$ is found by summing the rows in the table. So let's add the sum of each row to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.2}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.8}$ |\n\nNotice how we wrote the values of the *marginal* mass function in the right *margin* of the table.\n\nFrom here, we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\nP(X=1) = f_X(1) = 0.2, \\qquad P(X=2) = f_X(2) = 0.8.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can compute the marginal mass function of $Y,$ denoted $f_Y(y),$ by summing the columns. Adding these totals to our table, we get the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y={\\color{blue}{1}}$ | $Y={\\color{red}{2}}$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.8$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.75}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ | $1$ |\n\nAgain, notice how we wrote the values of the *marginal* mass function in the bottom *margin* of the table.\n\nFrom here, we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\nP(Y={\\color{blue}{1}}) = f_Y({\\color{blue}{1}}) = 0.75, \\qquad P(Y={\\color{red}{2}}) = f_Y({\\color{red}{2}}) = 0.25.\n$$\n\nFinally, the marginal mass function $f_Y(y)$ of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163331 #edb/ref 1065 6290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163331 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a1c9ecf8-619b-58b7-899f-b179dca8ae45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163331 #edb/ref 1067 "Deducing the Marginal Mass Function for Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163332 #edb/ref 1064 "Now that we've built some intuition, let's define the marginal mass functions formally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be the joint PMF of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively.\n\n- The **marginal mass function ** of $X,$ denoted $f_X(x),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = P(X=x)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{y \\in S_Y} f(x,y).\n$$\n\n- The **marginal mass function ** of $Y,$ denoted $f_Y(y),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_Y(y)= P(Y=y)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S_X} f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe can relate these definitions to our previous intuition:\n\n- The first definition states that, to compute $P(X=x)$ for some particular value $x,$ we sum all possible values of $Y$ in the row corresponding to $X=x.$\n\n- The second definition states that, to compute $P(Y=y)$ for some particular value $y,$ we sum all possible values of $X$ in the column corresponding to $Y=y.$\n\nFinally, note that marginal mass functions are sometimes called **marginal probability mass functions**, **marginal PMFs**, or (less formally) **marginal distributions**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163332 #edb/ref 1065 6291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163332 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddca7e0f-a315-5567-97d7-f5b40876617b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163332 #edb/ref 1067 "A Formal Definition of the Marginal Mass Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269839 #edb/ref 1080 "0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e6364f8-ede1-568c-9447-7d8512d55750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269945 #edb/ref 1080 "0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269945 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269945 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b199b00-2e32-51b7-a0a2-2af7edc30793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270457 #edb/ref 1080 "0.35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270457 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270457 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "89a2d1f9-b60b-5ec4-add3-d4cba31eb01d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270736 #edb/ref 1080 "0.45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270736 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270736 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a48bdf26-5f1a-5acc-91d7-652daba55fb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270752 #edb/ref 1080 "0.25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270752 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270752 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a59e14ce-d806-5a21-85ba-749c56d7d474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270792 #edb/ref 1080 "0.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270792 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270792 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aa7ab526-7b74-5841-a507-644509a22dcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270874 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270874 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270874 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b4316442-e48c-5a9f-970b-60c4e4e62eff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271165 #edb/ref 1080 "0.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271165 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271165 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d8176f53-7cdf-5ca4-a8a3-04aab032e066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271256 #edb/ref 1080 "0.65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271256 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271256 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e233ef36-ef94-5e6c-895a-e363b992f2f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271313 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271313 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271313 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e984de61-29d8-5c1f-b4c2-570abff33fd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271696 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "677b355b-9aaf-545c-9e9f-5ddacac81e3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186271256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271728 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7c1240f6-22a0-5c0f-bdb8-d662a62e2820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272356 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272356 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272356 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272356 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find ${f}_{Y}(2).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272356 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81d1721a-cc20-5d10-aa30-7f257189469f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272356 #edb/ref 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(0,2)+f(1,2) \\\\  & =0.2+0.05 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(|Y|>0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d18fe4f-c908-58f7-9a78-04f4bc6d6db6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272364 #edb/ref 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $Y$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|>0) & =P(Y\\in \\{-1,1\\}) \\\\  & =P(Y=-1)+P(Y=1) \\\\  & =0.2+0.45 \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
